  "text" : "In PyTorch, NumWorkers doesn't split your data across the workers.\nEach worker is a full copy of your dataset fetching and collating on its own.\nSo when you run an iterable dataset with multiple workers, every worker gets the same dataset\nobject and unless you split the stream yourself, they all deliver the same data.\nWith a map-style dataset, you're fine.\nThe sampler stays in the main process and hands each worker a different set of indices.\nEvery sample here gets fetched once.\nBut with an iterable dataset, there are no indices.\nworker starts the stream from the beginning. So if you have the same seed, you're getting\nthe same data. Two workers, batch size 4, the first two batches will be identical.\nThe fix lives inside your iter function. Ask for the worker info, and have worker k take\nevery nth sample, starting at k. Now the two copies can walk the stream in step and nothing\nis delivered twice. So before you increase numWorkers on an iterable dataset, be sure\nyou're passing that worker info to each iter function so you can split the stream accordingly."
